#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-source-root — the root a citation of a plugin-shipped FILE resolves
# against. Read at a skill's first step (skills/setup, skills/next,
# skills/cleanup, skills/help) in place of restating the branch inline.
#
# Usage:
#   fusion-source-root
#
# Output on stdout: exactly one line, the directory that a citation of
# `agents/…`, `skills/…`, `rules/…`, `docs/…` or a shipped `README*.md` is
# opened relative to.
#
#   $PWD                  when bin/fusion-plugin-cwd says the working directory
#                         IS the fusion plugin's own source repository
#   $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT   otherwise — the installed copy the SessionStart hook
#                         exported, which is what every consuming project sees
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  a root was resolved and printed.
#   1  usage error.
#   2  there is no root to print: FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT is unset or empty AND the
#      working directory is not the plugin's own repository, so there is
#      nothing to fall to. Nothing on stdout, the reason on stderr. A caller
#      reports the root as UNRESOLVED and reads NOTHING through it — an empty
#      value resolves every citation from `/`, which finds nothing and says
#      nothing about why.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this is one helper and not a line in each skill body
#
# The criterion below lived in four executable copies across two skill bodies
# (an announcing block and an inline re-resolution in each, because a later
# shell call is a fresh shell). A correction to one fact then had to be written
# into every copy, and getting it wrong in one was invisible: `63deec1` moved
# two skills onto the resolved root and left two more citing the install copy,
# which is issue 260811-0109. Repeated prose drifts in meaning; repeated shell
# drifts in behaviour. Decision 260810-2145, option 1.
#
# Why the branch at all. $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT names the installed copy and is
# pinned for the whole session. Inside the plugin's own source repository that
# is the wrong copy: bin/fusion-rules and bin/fusion-paths read the work tree
# there on purpose, so a citation left on the install would hand a reader rules
# and paths from the checkout and a cited prompt section from the install — two
# versions of one file, differing in silence.
#
# Why every documented call site guards the call with `[ -x ]`. The four skill
# bodies do not call this script bare; each tests it for executability first,
# and the guard is the one every prompt-called helper in this directory
# carries. The reason is not defensive habit: a helper added to the plugin's
# work tree between releases is simply absent from an older install, so a bare
# call there is exit 127 at the caller's own first step. The absent branch
# falls back to the install root, which is the behaviour that preceded this
# helper, and says so on stderr rather than resolving in silence. An unset
# FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT does not fall back anywhere, because there is nothing to
# fall to, and it is the branch a caller reports as UNRESOLVED.
#
# The no-upward-walk bound is inherited, not re-decided. bin/fusion-plugin-cwd
# tests the working directory and never walks up, so from a SUBDIRECTORY of
# that repository the answer is the install and every consumer then behaves as
# it does in a consuming project. This script adds no walk of its own. The
# bound used to be justified as congruence with hooks/lib/self-detect.ts; that
# module's cwd entry point was deleted on 2026-08-16 with the guard's
# stand-down, so the helper is now the sole cwd-anchored implementation and the
# bound stands on its own reason — a walk here would resolve a skill's citation
# against a repository root the caller never named. SessionStart says out loud
# when a session starts below the root (hooks/session-start.ts), which is the
# one condition under which this bound surprises anybody.
#
# What this helper does NOT root. A `bin/` executable is always run from
# $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, and so is an asset a skill copies (templates/, stilwerk/,
# bin/monitor, .claude-plugin/plugin.json). Whether the work-tree preference
# extends to helper resolution is part (c) of decision 260810-1544 and is
# explicitly UNANSWERED; it must not be assumed here. The split is by what the
# caller does with the path: read shipped text -> this root; run or copy an
# installed artefact -> $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT.
#
# Resolution of its own sibling. bin/fusion-plugin-cwd is found relative to THIS
# script rather than through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, the way bin/fusion-staging-drift
# resolves its own entry point, so an install copy and a work tree each ask
# their own criterion. Called the documented way — through
# "$FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT/bin/fusion-source-root" — the two are the same directory.
#
# One documented difference from the inline snippet this replaces. That snippet
# tested `-z FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT` FIRST, so an unset variable produced the empty
# root even inside the plugin's own repository. This script answers the
# plugin-cwd branch first, because that answer needs no install to be true. The
# difference is unreachable from the documented call sites: their guard is
# `[ -x "$FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT/bin/fusion-source-root" ]`, which an unset variable
# already fails, so they never reach this script to see it.

set -eu

if [ "$#" -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "usage: fusion-source-root" >&2
  exit 1
fi

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"

if [ -x "$here/fusion-plugin-cwd" ] && "$here/fusion-plugin-cwd"; then
  printf '%s\n' "$PWD"
  exit 0
fi

if [ -n "${FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT:-}" ]; then
  printf '%s\n' "$FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT"
  exit 0
fi

echo "fusion-source-root: FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT is unset and the working directory is not the fusion plugin's own source repository — there is no source root to print." >&2
exit 2
